SERVICES_MKDB(8)          BSD System Manager's Manual         SERVICES_MKDB(8)

NNAAMMEE
     sseerrvviicceess__mmkkddbb — generate the services database

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     sseerrvviicceess__mmkkddbb [--bb | --ll] [--qq] [--oo _d_a_t_a_b_a_s_e] [_f_i_l_e]
     sseerrvviicceess__mmkkddbb --uu [_f_i_l_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The sseerrvviicceess__mmkkddbb utility creates a db(3) database for the specified
     _f_i_l_e.  If no file is specified, then _/_e_t_c_/_s_e_r_v_i_c_e_s is used.  The database
     is installed into _/_v_a_r_/_d_b_/_s_e_r_v_i_c_e_s_._d_b.  The file must be in the correct
     format (see services(5)).

     The options are as follows:

     --bb      Use big-endian byte order for database metadata.

     --ll      Use little-endian byte order for database metadata.

     --oo _d_a_t_a_b_a_s_e
             Put the output databases in the named file.

     --qq      Do not warn about duplicate services.

     --uu      Print the services file to stdout, omitting duplicate entries and
             comments.

     The databases are used by the C library services routines (see
     getservent(3)).

     The --bb and --ll flags are mutually exclusive.  The default byte ordering is
     the current host order.

FFIILLEESS
     /var/db/services.db      The current services database.
     /var/db/services.db.tmp  A temporary file.
     /etc/services            The current services file.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The sseerrvviicceess__mmkkddbb utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     db(3), getservent(3), services(5)

BBUUGGSS
     Because sseerrvviicceess__mmkkddbb guarantees not to install a partial destination
     file it must build a temporary file in the same file system and if suc‐
     cessful use rename(2) to install over the destination file.

     If sseerrvviicceess__mmkkddbb fails it will leave the previous version of the destina‐
     tion file intact.

